          "id": "15708c6c7277efb820d4e0dc5889b656aaaa3c88",
          "message": "perf(dspark): run the confidence head as one fused GEMV (#3434)\n\n* perf(dspark): run the confidence head as one fused GEMV on gfx1250\n\nDSparkConfidenceHead concatenated h_k with the Markov embedding and ran an\nN=1 GEMM plus a sigmoid. At draft widths that GEMM tiles M and N by 128, so a\nhandful of workgroups stream the activations while almost every output lane\nis padding, and the concatenated copy costs about as much again.\n\nA Triton GEMV reads both operands where they are, accumulates each row in\nFP32 and applies the sigmoid in the same launch. It is taken for a bias-free\nBF16 head whose hidden width is a multiple of 512 and whose rank is a power\nof two, on gfx1250; everything else keeps the cat + GEMM + sigmoid path.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(dspark): take the fused confidence GEMV on every GPU arch\n\nReview follow-up. The arch gate is gone: the kernel is plain Triton and is\nfaster than cat + N=1 GEMM + sigmoid on gfx950 as well as gfx1250, so\n`can_fuse_confidence` checks only what the kernel needs (a bias-free BF16\nhead it tiles). That also drops the hand-rolled gcnArchName probe.\n\n`fused_confidence` now refuses a shape the unmasked kernel would read past\n(hidden not a multiple of the K block, a rank that is not a power of two,\nstrided rows or a mismatched head) with a ValueError, through the same\n`_fits_kernel` predicate the gate uses. The block sizes say why they are\nwhat they are, and the forward's comments sit next to the imports they\ndescribe; the fused path is \"FP32 accumulation\", not the GEMM's order.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
